# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Aṣẹ API — CI (DISABLED)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# This workflow is inert on purpose: no GitHub Actions / CI is set up for
# this repository yet, and the GitHub App currently lacks the `workflows`
# permission needed to push changes to this file.
#
# It is disabled two ways, deliberately:
#   * the filename ends in `.disabled` — GitHub only parses `.yml`/`.yaml`
#     directly inside `.github/workflows/`, so this file is ignored entirely;
#   * the contents are commented out as well.
#
# The rename matters: a comment-only `.yml` here parses to `null`, and
# GitHub reports that as an "Invalid workflow file" (red X in the Actions
# tab, usually an email too). The `.disabled` suffix avoids that noise.
#
# TO ENABLE
#   1. Grant the GitHub App the `workflows` permission (or commit this file
#      manually from an account that has it).
#   2. Rename this file to `ci.yml`.
#   3. Uncomment everything below this header — in most editors: select the
#      block and toggle the line comment. Nothing else needs changing.
#   4. Push. The first run tells you whether the suite is actually green;
#      as of writing it is NOT (see the TODO in DEVELOPMENT.md).
#
# WHY `--no-security-blocking` APPEARS BELOW
#   `laravel/framework` is pinned `^11.31`, and every version that constraint
#   can resolve to carries an unpatched advisory (incl. CVE-2026-48019).
#   Composer refuses to install such a set by default, so CI cannot install
#   dependencies without that flag. It is a deliberate, temporary escape
#   hatch — DELETE IT once the framework is upgraded to 12.60+/13.10+, so
#   that genuinely new advisories start failing the build again.
#
#   If you want advisories surfaced without blocking the build in the
#   meantime, the `composer audit` step below is left non-fatal
#   (`continue-on-error: true`) for exactly that purpose.
#
# Service versions and credentials mirror docker-compose.yml and the
# `<php>` env block in phpunit.xml — keep them in sync.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

